{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ad40c1bdf84c709b","type":"tool_result","content":"# Platform: RHOAI 3.6-ea.1\n\n## Metadata\n\n- **Distribution**: RHOAI\n- **Version**: 3.6-ea.1\n- **Base Platform**: OpenShift Container Platform v4.x\n- **Supported OCP Versions**: v4.16+\n- **Supported CPU Architectures**: amd64\n- **Total Shipped Container Images**: 72+\n- **Components Analyzed**: 72\n- **Generated By**: Claude Opus 4.6 on 2026-08-13\n\n## Platform Overview\n\nRed Hat OpenShift AI (RHOAI) 3.6-ea.1 is a comprehensive AI/ML platform deployed on OpenShift Container Platform through the Operator Lifecycle Manager (OLM). The platform is orchestrated by rhods-operator, which manages the lifecycle of all platform components through a hierarchy of custom resources — DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, and per-component CRDs. Each major capability (model serving, pipelines, distributed workloads, notebooks, model registry, training, observability, guardrails, and models-as-a-service) is implemented by a dedicated module operator or controller that watches its component CR and reconciles downstream Kubernetes resources using kustomize-based manifest rendering through the shared odh-platform-utilities framework.\n\nThe platform ingress architecture is in active transition. The rhods-operator Gateway service controller provisions a central `data-science-gateway` using Gateway API (GatewayClass, Gateway, HTTPRoutes) while maintaining backward compatibility through OpenShift Routes. kube-auth-proxy provides OIDC-authenticated access to the dashboard, and the Gateway controller conditionally integrates with Istio by dynamically creating EnvoyFilter and DestinationRule resources when Istio CRDs are detected. Models-as-a-Service introduces a parallel ingress path through Kuadrant AuthPolicy enforcement with API key, TokenReview, and optional OIDC JWT authentication at the Gateway level. Model-registry-operator implements dual ingress (Gateway API HTTPRoutes and OpenShift Routes) with kube-rbac-proxy sidecar authentication on backend pods.\n\nSecurity follows a defense-in-depth posture: all shipped Go binaries are built with `CGO_ENABLED=1` and `GOEXPERIMENT=strictfipsruntime` for FIPS-compliant cryptography through system OpenSSL, operators read the cluster APIServer TLS security profile to align their TLS configuration with platform-wide policy, admission webhooks validate and default CRD resources with fail-closed policies, and per-component RBAC is scoped through dedicated ServiceAccounts and ClusterRoles. Authentication is layered across gateway-level (kube-auth-proxy OIDC, Kuadrant AuthPolicy), pod-level (kube-rbac-proxy sidecar), and application-level (Kubernetes TokenReview, SubjectAccessReview) enforcement points.\n\n## Component Relationships\n\n### Dependency Graph\n\n```\nrhods-operator\n|-- kserve (runtime-integration)\n|   `-- kube-rbac-proxy (unknown)\n|-- model-registry (unknown)\n|   `-- kserve (runtime-integration) (*)\n`-- models-as-a-service (runtime-library)\n    `-- kserve (runtime-integration) (*)\n\nLeaf components (no internal deps to/from other components):\n  MLServer\n  NeMo-Guardrails\n  argo-workflows\n  eval-hub\n  fms-guardrails-orchestrator\n  guardrails-regex-detector\n  kube-auth-proxy\n  llm-d-planner\n  lm-evaluation-harness\n  mcp-lifecycle-module-operator\n  mcp-lifecycle-operator\n  ml-metadata\n  model-metadata-collection\n  odh-cli\n  odh-observability\n  ogx-k8s-operator\n  OpenShell\n  rhaii-cluster-validation\n  spark-operator\n  trainer\n  trainer-operator\n  training_hub\n  training-operator\n  vllm-cpu\n  workload-variant-autoscaler\n```\n\n### Central Components\n\n**rhods-operator** is the platform root: it owns all component CRDs (DataSciencePipelines, Kueue, ModelRegistry, Ray, SparkOperator, Trainer, TrainingOperator, TrustyAI) and three service controllers (Auth, Gateway, Monitoring) that manage cross-cutting platform infrastructure. Every component operator depends on rhods-operator for activation and lifecycle management.\n\n**kserve** is the most connected serving component with 13 CRDs, 4 independent controller binaries (manager, llmisvc, localmodel, kserve-module), and integration with Gateway API, KEDA, cert-manager, Istio, and the Gateway API Inference Extension. Both rhods-operator and model-registry depend on it.\n\n**odh-model-controller** serves as the serving control plane companion to kserve, managing 10 reconcilers covering InferenceService, InferenceGraph, LLMInferenceService, and Gateway resources. It provides the model-serving-api REST endpoint and manages Kuadrant AuthPolicies and KEDA autoscaling for the serving layer.\n\n**odh-dashboard** is the primary user interface, implementing a micro-frontend architecture with Webpack Module Federation. It watches platform CRDs (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, KServe InferenceService, ModelRegistry, MLflow, TrustyAI, Feast) to reflect real-time platform state.\n\n**models-as-a-service** is the multi-tenant model serving gateway, providing OpenAI-compatible API access with Kuadrant-based authentication and subscription governance.\n\n### Integration Patterns\n\n| Pattern | Components Using | Description |\n|---------|------------------|-------------|\n| CRD-based orchestration | rhods-operator, kserve, odh-model-controller, models-as-a-service, kuberay, kueue-operator, trainer-operator, workbenches-operator, spark-operator, model-registry-operator, feast-module-operator, mlflow-operator, ai-gateway-operator, ogx-k8s-operator, trustyai-service-operator, data-science-pipelines-operator, mcp-lifecycle-operator, mcp-lifecycle-module-operator, llm-d-batch-gateway-operator | Each component defines cluster-scoped CRDs watched by the platform operator; component operators reconcile their own CRDs into namespace-scoped workloads |\n| ODH module operator pattern | kserve (kserve-module), ai-gateway-operator, trainer-operator, workbenches-operator, feast-module-operator, mlflow-operator, mcp-lifecycle-module-operator, ogx-k8s-operator | Module operators embed odh-platform-utilities framework for standardized manifest rendering, deploy actions, garbage collection, and status reporting |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b82c88193c77520","agent_id":"ae447954eeaf34fb2","session_id":"f2d69f1c-9b04-453b-94d6-4bb7c2d36986","uuid":"49762911-025f-4292-bda3-4c73f972495d","timestamp":"2026-10-22T07:17:46.718Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-ad40c1bdf84c709b","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8001"}
