{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a30999cd66b07b71","type":"tool_result","content":"=== notebooks ===\n- **Languages**: Go, Python\n**Short**: Multi-variant container image build repository that produces JupyterLab notebook and code-server workbench images for RHOAI, each bundling a specific ML framework stack (PyTorch, TensorFlow, LLMCompressor) on UBI9. [source: jupyter/minimal/ubi9-python-3.12/Dockerfile.konflux.cpu:1, jupyter/minimal/ubi9-python-3.12/start-notebook.sh:48, codeserver/ubi9-python-3.12/Dockerfile.konflux.cpu:367]\n**Detailed**: The notebooks repository builds the container images that serve as RHOAI workbenches — interactive development environments for data scientists. It produces two IDE families: JupyterLab notebooks (started via `start-notebook.sh`) and code-server instances (started via `run-code-server.sh`), each available in multiple variants combining a base Python environment with accelerator-specific ML frameworks (CPU, NVIDIA CUDA, AMD ROCm). The images are built through Konflux pipelines using UBI9 base images with hermetic RPM and Python dependency management. Each image bundles a curated set of data science libraries including PyTorch, TensorFlow, scikit-learn, pandas, and platform SDKs such as codeflare-sdk and kfp. The workbench images are deployed as StatefulSets managed by odh-notebook-controller, which injects environment variables (`NOTEBOOK_ARGS`, `NB_PREFIX`) for routing and an oauth-proxy sidecar for authentication. The repository also includes Go-based build utilities (`buildinputs`, `check-payload`) used during the Konflux image build pipeline, and a comprehensive container test suite validating image correctness including idle culling API behavior. [source: jupyter/minimal/ubi9-python-3.12/start-notebook.sh:1-51, jupyter/minimal/ubi9-python-3.12/kustomize/base/statefulset.yaml:1-65, codeserver/ubi9-python-3.12/Dockerfile.konflux.cpu:367, tests/containers/workbenches/culling_api_test.py:32-33]\nThe notebooks repository follows a multi-variant image build pattern where a shared base layer is extended by framework-specific Dockerfiles. Each JupyterLab variant uses `start-notebook.sh` as its entrypoint, which reads platform-injected environment variables (`NOTEBOOK_PORT`, `NOTEBOOK_BASE_URL`, `NOTEBOOK_ROOT_DIR`, `NOTEBOOK_ARGS`) to configure the JupyterLab `ServerApp` at startup. [source: jupyter/minimal/ubi9-python-3.12/start-notebook.sh:16-51] The code-server variants use a parallel `run-code-server.sh` entrypoint with an nginx reverse-proxy and httpd CGI layer that exposes a `/api/kernels/` culling shim, enabling the platform's idle-detection mechanism to query workbench activity. [source: tests/containers/workbenches/culling_api_test.py:87-135]\nAuthentication is architecturally delegated: the kustomize StatefulSet template explicitly disables JupyterLab's built-in token and password authentication (`--ServerApp.token=''`, `--ServerApp.password=''`), expecting the platform to provide authentication via a sidecar proxy injected by odh-notebook-controller. [source: jupyter/minimal/ubi9-python-3.12/kustomize/base/statefulset.yaml:26-29] This design means the workbench images themselves contain no authentication enforcement — a deliberate architectural choice that makes them dependent on correct platform deployment.\nThe Go utilities (`buildinputs` and `check-payload`) are build-pipeline tools, not runtime components of the deployed workbench. Both are statically linked (`CGO_ENABLED=0`) and built from minimal base images (`scratch` and `ubi9-minimal` respectively), serving as Konflux pipeline helpers rather than as part of the deployed workbench pod. [source: scripts/buildinputs/Dockerfile:17, scripts/check-payload/Dockerfile:18]\nFor FIPS compliance, the notebook images install `compat-openssl11` to provide `libcrypto.so.1.1` required by FIPS-compliance scanning tools. [source: jupyter/minimal/ubi9-python-3.12/Dockerfile.konflux.cpu:27-30] The Python `cryptography` library (v49.0.0) uses the system OpenSSL backend on UBI9. However, `pycryptodome` (v3.23.0) is also present in the dependency tree and is not a FIPS-validated cryptographic provider. The Go build utilities use `CGO_ENABLED=0` static linking, which would not pass FIPS check-payload validation, but these are build-time tools not shipped in the runtime workbench images.\n| codeserver-baseline/ubi9-python-3.12/Dockerfile.konflux.cpu:CMD | Container entrypoint | [\"/opt/app-root/bin/run-code-server.sh\"] |\n| codeserver/ubi9-python-3.12/Dockerfile.konflux.cpu:CMD | Container entrypoint | [\"/opt/app-root/bin/run-code-server.sh\"] |\n| datascience-notebook | Python Package | Python package or application |\n| examples/jupyterlab-with-elyra/Dockerfile:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/opt/app-root/bin/start-notebook.sh\"] |\n| gunicorn | Python ASGI/WSGI server | Python entrypoint |\n=== odh-dashboard ===\nThe dashboard-operator reconciles Dashboard custom resources created by the rhods-operator, deploying and configuring the console pod alongside its BFF sidecars. The operator implements a declarative ownership model where the parent Dashboard CR controls the lifecycle of Deployments, Services, Routes, NetworkPolicies, PodDisruptionBudgets, and RBAC resources via controller-runtime's `Owns()` watches. Controller watches on platform CRDs (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, KServe InferenceService, ModelRegistry, MLflow, TrustyAI, Feast) enable the dashboard to reflect the real-time state of the AI platform without polling. The operator uses `odh-platform-utilities` for platform detection, manifest rendering, and deployment helpers. [source: dashboard-operator/charts/dashboard/values.yaml:15-18, dashboard-operator/go.mod:6, dashboard-operator/config/rbac/role.yaml:1]\nThe component packages a Workspace controller (reconciling kubeflow.org/v1beta1 Workspace and WorkspaceKind resources with validating and conversion webhooks) alongside the dashboard operator. Workspaces are managed via StatefulSets with associated Services and PodDisruptionBudgets. Cert-manager provisions TLS certificates for the webhook and metrics endpoints. All Go binaries (operator and BFF sidecars) are built with `CGO_ENABLED=1` and `-tags strictfipsruntime` for FIPS-compliant cryptographic operations through system OpenSSL. [source: Dockerfile.konflux.dashboard-operator:18-20, Dockerfile.konflux.genai:86, packages/notebooks/upstream/workspaces/controller/internal/webhook/workspace_webhook.go:44]\n| Dockerfile.konflux.agent-ops:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n| Dockerfile.konflux.automl:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n| Dockerfile.konflux.autorag:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n| Dockerfile.konflux.core-bff:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n| Dockerfile.konflux.dashboard-operator:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/manager\"] |\n| Dockerfile.konflux.eval-hub:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n| Dockerfile.konflux.genai:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n| Dockerfile.konflux.maas:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n| Dockerfile.konflux.mlflow:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n| Dockerfile.konflux.modelregistry:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n=== trustyai-explainability ===\n**Short**: TrustyAI Explainability Service is a Quarkus-based Java service that provides model fairness monitoring, data drift detection, and explainability for ML models served by KServe/ModelMesh on the RHOAI platform. [source: explainability-service/src/main/resources/application.properties:102-118, explainability-service/manifests/base/trustyai-deployment.yaml:27-145]\n**Detailed**: TrustyAI Explainability Service is a Quarkus 3.8 application deployed as a Kubernetes Deployment that integrates with the KServe/ModelMesh model serving stack to provide AI model trustworthiness capabilities. It consumes inference payloads from ModelMesh via a dedicated `/consumer/kserve/v2` endpoint, storing input/output data for subsequent analysis. The service exposes REST endpoints for computing fairness metrics (Statistical Parity Difference, Disparate Impact Ratio), data drift detection (Meanshift, KS Test, Fourier MMD, Approximate KS Test), and service metadata. In the RHOAI distribution, fairness and drift endpoints are enabled while local and global explainer endpoints (SHAP, LIME, Counterfactual, TSSaliency) are disabled via Quarkus profile feature flags. The service supports optional database persistence via MariaDB/MySQL through Hibernate ORM (disabled by default), with PVC-based file storage as the primary data backend. Metrics are exposed to Prometheus at `/q/metrics` via service annotations, and the service supports TLS on port 4443 when certificates are provisioned at `/etc/tls/internal/`. [source: explainability-service/src/main/resources/application.properties:1-132, explainability-service/manifests/base/trustyai-deployment.yaml:2-185, explainability-service/src/main/java/org/kie/trustyai/service/endpoints/consumer/ConsumerEndpoint.java:29-32]\nTrustyAI Explainability Service follows a payload-consumer architecture where it passively receives inference data from the KServe/ModelMesh model serving infrastructure. An init container in the Deployment creates a `model-serving-config` ConfigMap that configures ModelMesh's payload processor to forward inference inputs and outputs to the TrustyAI service's `/consumer/kserve/v2` endpoint within the same namespace. This tight coupling means TrustyAI must be deployed in the same namespace as the model serving workloads it monitors. [source: explainability-service/manifests/base/trustyai-deployment.yaml:62-81]\nData persistence uses a dual-mode design: PVC-based file storage is the default (via `trustyai-service-pvc`), with optional MariaDB/MySQL persistence available through Hibernate ORM configuration that is disabled by default (`QUARKUS_HIBERNATE_ORM_ACTIVE` defaults to `false`). The ClusterRole grants the service account broad ConfigMap permissions (create, delete, get, list, patch, update, watch) at cluster scope, which is used by the init container's `oc apply` command and by the service itself for configuration management. [source: explainability-service/manifests/base/trustyai-deployment.yaml:141-151, 159-175, explainability-service/src/main/resources/application.properties:63-72]\n| explainability-service/src/main/docker/Dockerfile.native-micro:CMD | Container entrypoint | [\"./application\", \"-Dquarkus.http.host=0.0.0.0\"] |\n| explainability-service/src/main/docker/Dockerfile.native:CMD | Container entrypoint | [\"./application\", \"-Dquarkus.http.host=0.0.0.0\"] |\n| tests/Dockerfile:CMD | Container entrypoint | $HOME/peak/installandtest.sh |\n| Name | Kind | API Group | Version | Scope | Supported Model Formats | Container Images | Built-in Adapter | Source |\n| Path | Method | Port | Protocol | Transport | Encryption | Auth | Owner | Purpose |\n| Service | Port | Protocol | Transport | Encryption | Auth | Owner | Purpose |\n| KServe/ModelMesh | API consumer | data-source | Receives inference payloads via /consumer/kserve/v2; init container configures model-serving-config ConfigMap |\nThis is a Java/Quarkus application running on UBI9 OpenJDK 17 runtime. FIPS compliance for Java applications on RHEL/UBI relies on the JDK using the system-level FIPS crypto providers (NSS/OpenSSL) when the host operates in FIPS mode. No explicit FIPS build flags or annotations are present.\n=== workbenches-operator ===\nThe workbenches-operator follows a single-CR reconciler pattern: one cluster-scoped `Workbenches` resource controls the entire component lifecycle. The reconciler applies upstream Kubeflow Notebook Controller manifests via kustomize server-side apply, setting owner references on all created resources (ConfigMaps, Secrets, Services, ServiceAccounts, Deployments, RBAC objects, and webhook configurations) so that deletion cascades cleanly through `reconcileDelete`. A finalizer guards teardown to ensure webhook configurations and CRDs are removed before the CR disappears. [source: internal/controller/workbenches_controller.go:111-139, 146-160]\nTLS posture is driven by the OpenShift APIServer TLS security profile via `controller-runtime-common/pkg/tls`. At startup, the operator fetches the cluster profile and adherence policy; on non-OpenShift clusters or transient API errors, it falls back to TLS 1.2 minimum with Mozilla Intermediate ciphers (all FIPS-approved). A `SecurityProfileWatcher` cancels the manager context on profile changes, triggering a pod restart to adopt the new configuration. The metrics endpoint at `:8443` uses the same TLS options and gates access through controller-runtime's `WithAuthenticationAndAuthorization` filter (TokenReview + SubjectAccessReview). [source: cmd/main.go:120-146, 223-228, internal/tlsconfig/tlsconfig.go:41-48, 107-154]\n| Dockerfile.konflux:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/manager\"] |\n| Dockerfile:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/manager\"] |\n| Name | Kind | API Group | Version | Scope | Supported Model Formats | Container Images | Built-in Adapter | Source |\n| Path | Method | Port | Protocol | Transport | Encryption | Auth | Owner | Purpose |\n| Service | Port | Protocol | Transport | Encryption | Auth | Owner | Purpose |\n| **OpenSSL in image** | Yes (via UBI9-minimal base) | Dockerfile.konflux:20 |\n| **Crypto libraries** | stdlib crypto/tls (FIPS-compliant via OpenSSL backend with strictfipsruntime tag) | internal/tlsconfig/tlsconfig.go:24 |\nThe build uses `strictfipsruntime` which enforces FIPS-validated cryptographic routines at runtime via Go's OpenSSL backend when running on a FIPS-enabled host. The TLS configuration is profile-driven on OpenShift, meaning it inherits whatever cipher suite the cluster administrator has configured through the APIServer TLS security profile. On non-OpenShift clusters, the operator uses a hardened Mozilla Intermediate cipher set that excludes legacy and non-FIPS algorithms. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:16-17, internal/tlsconfig/tlsconfig.go:41-48, 107-154]\n| e024eba | 2026-08-12 | chore(deps): update registry.redhat.io/ubi9/go-toolset docker digest to 444e81b (#47) |\n=== rhods-operator ===\n**Detailed**: rhods-operator serves as the central control plane for Red Hat OpenShift AI (RHOAI). It defines and reconciles a hierarchy of custom resources—DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, and per-component CRDs such as DataSciencePipelines, Kueue, ModelRegistry, Ray, and KServe—that declaratively describe the desired state of the AI/ML platform. The operator deploys and manages each component's manifests via kustomize-based rendering and template actions, while a set of service controllers handle cross-cutting concerns: the Auth service controller manages RBAC group policies and watches for dependent namespaces (models-as-a-service, kuadrant-system), the Gateway service controller provisions a data-science gateway with kube-auth-proxy OIDC infrastructure and conditionally integrates with Istio (EnvoyFilter, DestinationRule) and OpenShift OAuth, and the Monitoring service controller deploys Prometheus proxies and collectors for platform-level observability. A companion cloudmanager binary handles cloud-provider Kubernetes engine resources (AWS, Azure, CoreWeave). The operator exposes 19 CRDs, serves admission webhooks on port 9443 with service-ca TLS, and reads the cluster APIServer TLS profile to align its own TLS configuration with the platform-wide security policy. [source: Dockerfiles/Dockerfile.konflux:56-59, cmd/main.go:480-494, internal/controller/services/auth/auth_controller.go:56-88, internal/controller/services/gateway/gateway_controller.go:40-109, pkg/tls/profile.go:151-162]\nThe Auth service controller manages a template-driven RBAC system that provisions admin-group and allowed-group cluster roles and role bindings. It watches for the creation and deletion of the `models-as-a-service` and `kuadrant-system` namespaces to trigger reconciliation, establishing runtime coupling with the MaaS and Kuadrant subsystems without direct API dependency. [source: internal/controller/services/auth/auth_controller.go:56-88]\nSecurity enforcement follows a defense-in-depth pattern: admission webhooks validate and default DataScienceCluster and DSCInitialization resources with a fail-closed policy, the metrics endpoint uses Kubernetes authentication and authorization filters when secure serving is enabled, and all webhook traffic is encrypted via OpenShift service-ca provisioned TLS certificates. The operator builds with `GOEXPERIMENT=strictfipsruntime` and `CGO_ENABLED=1`, ensuring FIPS-compliant cryptographic primitives on RHEL hosts. [source: Dockerfiles/Dockerfile.konflux:56-59, cmd/main.go:483-494]\n| Dockerfiles/Dockerfile.konflux:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/manager\"] |\n| Dockerfiles/Dockerfile:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/manager\"] |\n| Name | Kind | API Group | Version | Scope | Supported Model Formats | Container Images | Built-in Adapter | Source |\n| Path | Method | Port | Protocol | Transport | Encryption | Auth | Owner | Purpose |\n| Service | Port | Protocol | Transport | Encryption | Auth | Owner | Purpose |\n| github.com/google/go-containerregistry | v0.21.8 | Yes |  | Go module dependency |\n| github.com/opendatahub-io/models-as-a-service/maas-controller | v0.0.0-20260420142354-89fba298f42a | Yes |  | Go module dependency |\n| models-as-a-service | Go library | runtime-library | Use runtime packages from github.com/opendatahub-io/models-as-a-service/maas-controller |\n| Istio Service Mesh | Dynamic resource ownership | runtime-integration | Conditional EnvoyFilter and DestinationRule when Istio CRDs are present |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b82c88193c77520","agent_id":"ae447954eeaf34fb2","session_id":"f2d69