{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-0a360dd7-b468-4bdc-9de7-2384fb4bf64f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-bb2522a7a7a33936","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8001-sg79b66w/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8001-E010.md","content":"# fms-guardrails-orchestrator ML-KEM TLS stack migration\n\n## Description\n\nMigrate the fms-guardrails-orchestrator service off its rustls+ring TLS stack so it can negotiate ML-KEM (FIPS 203) hybrid key exchange, as required by the strategy's security NFR for non-Go components using pure-Rust TLS stacks. The service cannot inherit PQC from a base image swap because OpenSSL is not present in its release image; the migration is the remediation path behind the TLS migration spike RHOAIENG-7452. Until the migration lands, the team's September 15, 2026 compliance is met via an OCP exception with this migration as the remediation plan.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Resolve the TLS migration approach per the RHOAIENG-7452 spike outcome (crate/provider selection for an ML-KEM-capable Rust TLS stack, e.g. an aws-lc-rs-backed rustls provider or equivalent).\n- Migrate the service's TLS surfaces (inbound TLS/mTLS on the ingress surface, downstream REST and gRPC client connections) to the ML-KEM-capable stack, across all build variants (amd64, ppc64le, s390x).\n- Preserve existing TLS configuration semantics (per-service certificate and CA bundles, optional TLS/mTLS flags, header passthrough behavior).\n- Verify hybrid key exchange backwards compatibility: non-PQC TLS 1.3 clients continue to connect via classic ECDHE.\n- If the migration cannot complete by September 15, 2026, the OCP exception (with this migration as the documented remediation plan) remains the compliance path — the exception filing itself is owned by the team.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The service's release image uses an ML-KEM-capable TLS stack on all architectures; hybrid X25519MLKEM768 key exchange is negotiated with PQC-capable peers.\n- Non-PQC TLS 1.3 clients continue to connect (opportunistic hybrid negotiation, classic ECDHE fallback verified).\n- Existing TLS/mTLS configuration options behave as before (no HA or configuration regressions; no additional replicas required).\n- Until merged, the team's OCP exception documents this migration as the remediation plan, including the rustls+ring TLS stack as the reason ML-KEM cannot currently be negotiated.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 [P0]: All RHOAI runtime images must use a PQC-enabled base image — this epic delivers the \"or equivalent\" ML-KEM capability for a component a base image swap cannot remediate.\n- HLR-2 [P0]: Teams unable to comply by September 15 must file an OCP exception — the exception's remediation plan is this migration.\n- NFR (Security — PQC algorithm compliance, non-Go components): fms-guardrails-orchestrator must support ML-KEM hybrid key exchange after migration.\n\n## Risks\n\n- The migration approach depends on the RHOAIENG-7452 spike outcome and on ML-KEM support maturity in the Rust TLS ecosystem (external dependency).\n- Multi-architecture builds (ppc64le, s390x) constrain provider selection.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":63143,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b82c88193c77520","agent_id":"ae447954eeaf34fb2","session_id":"f2d69f1c-9b04-453b-94d6-4bb7c2d36986","uuid":"7a533906-596d-4934-9c55-5d0cda981799","timestamp":"2026-10-22T07:25:04.558Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8001"}
