{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-802bd862f05d971d","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8001\n3\tepic_count: 15\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8001 (Base image compliance — Post-quantum cryptography support)\n11\t\n12\tParent strategy: RHAISTRAT-8001 — RHOAI switch to PQC-enabled runtime base images for RHOAI 3.6 / OCP 5.0, deadline September 15, 2026.\n13\t\n14\t## Triage\n15\t\n16\tNot below-threshold: although the effort estimate is S, the strategy spans all RHOAI component images across many teams (Impacted Teams lists all RHOAI component teams, the AIPCC team, and DevOps/Build), and it contains genuine unknowns (PQC variants for python/nodejs bases, trainer data cache TLS posture, AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase timing) whose answers change which epics exist or what they do. Not docs-only: active code/build changes across components.\n17\t\n18\t## Step 1.5 — Staff Engineer Input\n19\t\n20\tThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section is empty (template placeholder only). No overrides to log.\n21\t\n22\t## Epic List\n23\t\n24\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n25\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E001 | Confirm PQC base image variants for python-311/312, python-312-minimal, nodejs-22 | Investigation | AIPCC Base Images | P0 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E002 | Audit trainer data cache Rust TLS stack posture | Investigation | Model Training | P0 |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E003 | RHEL 9.8 ELS rebase timing and rhel9-8-els adoption go/no-go | Investigation | AIPCC | P0 |\n29\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E004 | PQC base image pipeline and build configuration updates | Implementation | DevOps Build | P0 |\n30\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E005 | Switch ubi9-minimal-based platform images to ubi9-minimal-pqc | Implementation | DevOps Build (coordinated wave) | P0 |\n31\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E006 | Switch notebook and workbench images to rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal | Implementation | Notebooks | P0 |\n32\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E007 | Switch serving and training runtime images to rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal | Implementation | Model Serving | P0 |\n33\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E008 | Switch python-variant-based images to PQC python base variants | Implementation | Model Serving | P0 |\n34\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E009 | Dashboard nodejs-22 image PQC remediation | Implementation | Dashboard | P0 |\n35\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E010 | fms-guardrails-orchestrator ML-KEM TLS stack migration | Implementation | AI Guardrails | P1 |\n36\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E011 | Trainer data cache PQC remediation | Implementation | Model Training | P1 |\n37\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E012 | PQC compliance verification CI scan for the RHOAI 3.6 payload | Implementation | DevOps Build | P0 |\n38\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E013 | TLS handshake performance regression validation | Implementation | PerfScale | P1 |\n39\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E014 | PQC compliance coordination and tracking | Implementation | DevOps Build / PM | P0 |\n40\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E015 | PQC Readiness Matrix and base image switch documentation | Implementation (docs-authoring) | Documentation | P2 |\n41\t\n42\t## Dependency DAG\n43\t\n44\t```mermaid\n45\tgraph TD\n46\tE001[RHAISTRAT-8001-E001] --> E008\n47\tE001 --> E009\n48\tE002[RHAISTRAT-8001-E002] --> E011\n49\tE003[RHAISTRAT-8001-E003] --> E006\n50\tE003 --> E007\n51\tE004[RHAISTRAT-8001-E004] --> E015\n52\tE005[RHAISTRAT-8001-E005] --> E015\n53\tE006 --> E015\n54\tE007 --> E015\n55\tE008 --> E015\n56\tE009 --> E015\n57\tE010[RHAISTRAT-8001-E010] --> E015\n58\tE011 --> E015\n59\tE012[RHAISTRAT-8001-E012] --> E015\n60\tE013[RHAISTRAT-8001-E013] --> E015\n61\tE014[RHAISTRAT-8001-E014] --> E015\n62\t```\n63\t\n64\tArrows point from dependency to dependent (`E003 --> E006` means E003 must complete before E006 starts).\n65\t\n66\t## DAG Justification\n67\t\n68\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n69\t|------|------|-----------|\n70\t| E001 → E008 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates) | Whether PQC python base variants exist (and by when) determines whether the python-variant-based images can switch or must go the OCP-exception path. True gate: `remove` if variants are not available in time. |\n71\t| E001 → E009 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates) | The dashboard nodejs-22 base is \"PQC variant or exception\"; the variant-availability outcome selects switch vs exception. True gate: `remove` (exception fallback). |\n72\t| E002 → E011 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates) | The TLS-stack audit determines whether the trainer data cache inherits PQC from the base image (no remediation) or needs TLS-stack remediation / an OCP exception. True gate: `add_remediation`. |\n73\t| E003 → E006 | Rules 3, 16, 18 | The AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase (AIPCC-7258) is an external dependency with uncertain timing that gates rhel9-8-els adoption; the go/no-go decision determines whether the notebook/workbench images switch or file exceptions. Tier 2 work. |\n74\t| E003 → E007 | Rules 3, 16, 18 | Same rebase gate for the serving/training runtime images currently on rhel9-6-els. |\n75\t| all Implementation epics (E004–E014) → E015 | Rule 11 (docs last) | The PQC Readiness Matrix and documentation describe what was built; docs-authoring is blocked by every Implementation epic. |\n76\t| E004 ∥ E005 (no edge) | Rules 13, 14 | Base image availability is already confirmed (registry images exist); build-pipeline consumability is a configuration reference — an AC gate on the switch epics, not a DAG dependency. |\n77\t| E005 ∥ E012 (no edge) | Rule 15 | The verification scan tooling is built in parallel with the switches; first meaningful execution is gated by the scan's own AC (switched images present in the payload). |\n78\t| E005 ∥ E006/E007/E008/E009 (no edge) | Rules 14, 15 | Different image groups/repos; PQC base availability is a confirmed configuration input, not a build-against artifact. |\n79\t| E010, E013, E014 roots (no edges) | Rules 14, 17 | The fms-guardrails TLS migration, performance validation, and compliance coordination deliver independent value and are not gated by any in-strategy epic (RHOAIENG-7452 spike and RHAISTRAT-7816 are external). |\n80\t\n81\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n82\t\n83\t| HLR | Priority | Epics |\n84\t|-----|----------|-------|\n85\t| All RHOAI runtime images must use a PQC-enabled base image (ubi9-minimal-pqc, rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal, or equivalent) for RHOAI 3.6 / OCP 5.0 | P0 | E004 (enables), E005 (ubi9-minimal wave, ~68 images), E006 (notebooks/workbenches), E007 (runtimes/mlserver), E008 (python-based images), E009 (dashboard), E010 (fms-guardrails remediation), E011 (trainer data cache remediation); verified by E012 |\n86\t| Teams unable to comply by September 15 must file an OCP exception with a remediation plan | P0 | E014 (aggregated tracking, zero unaccounted images) + exception-fallback acceptance criteria on E006, E008, E009, E010, E011. Individual exception filings are owned by each team and are explicitly out of strategy scope. |\n87\t| PQC enablement is runtime only — only the final stage of the Dockerfile/Containerfile changes; no builder image change | P0 | E005 (primary: runtime-stage-only scope constraint); the same constraint is carried in the scope of E006–E009 |\n88\t| PQC-signed X.509 certificates (ML-DSA) | Deferred | Out of scope — no epic (follow-on phase per strategy) |\n89\t\n90\tFull coverage: no orphaned HLRs. HLR-4 is explicitly deferred by the strategy.\n91\t\n92\t## Tiered Delivery\n93\t\n94\tExternal dependencies with uncertain timing (AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase, python/nodejs PQC variants) allow partial value without them:\n95\t\n96\t- **Tier 1 (independent, start immediately)**: E001, E002, E003 (investigations), E004 (pipeline/build config), E005 (~68 ubi9-minimal-based images on the confirmed `ubi9/ubi-minimal-pqc`), E010, E011 (remediations), E012 (verification scan), E013 (performance validation), E014 (coordination).\n97\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated)**: E006, E007 (gated by the RHEL 9.8 rebase go/no-go), E008, E009 (gated by PQC variant availability). If a gate resolves negatively, the affected images fall back to the OCP-exception path (HLR-2) tracked by E014 — no unaccounted images.\n98\t\n99\t## Component Mapping Notes\n100\t\n101\tCanonical component names taken verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`:\n102\t\n103\t- **E005** spans many strategy components (Go operators and platform components, ~68 images). Assigned the single most-relevant canonical name `OpenShift AI Productization`; per-team execution is delegated via tracking tickets linked to the parent strategy per the strategy's own coordination model.\n104\t- **E007/E008** span serving and training runtime images (mlserver, training runtimes, training variants, lmes-job, feature-server). Assigned `Model Runtimes`; the feature-server image is Feature Store territory and lmes-job is AI Evaluations territory, but a single image each does not warrant separate epics.\n105\t- **E002/E011** (trainer / trainer-operator / trainer data cache): the canonical list has no \"Trainer\" component; mapped to `Model Customization` as the closest canonical name covering model training work.\n106\t- **E014** (coordination) mapped to `AI Platform DevOps`.\n107\t\n108\t## Health Warnings\n109\t\n110\t- **Performance NFR lacks a numeric threshold** — the strategy flags \"NEEDS DOMAIN INPUT\" (owner: PM + Performance team lead, due before Sprint 2 ends). Captured as an acceptance criterion on E013 (threshold agreed and documented before measurement); flagged for human verification.\n111\t- **TrustyAI explainability has no remediation epic** — Java/NSS TLS stack, remediation tied to the JDK 29 LTS timeline (September 2027). Per the strategy an OCP exception is expected; the filing is team-owned and out of scope. Compliance for this image is covered only via E014 tracking — a multi-release compliance gap worth leadership visibility.\n112\t- **Priority judgment call (possible inversion)**: E010 (fms-guardrails) and E011 (trainer data cache) remediations are P1 although HLR-1 is P0. Rationale: the September 15 deadline for these components is met via the HLR-2 exception path; the migrations are the remediation those exceptions commit to. If product decides the migrations must land in 3.6, raise both to P0.\n113\t- **FIPS + PQC incompatibility**: on FIPS-mode clusters, FIPS policy deactivates PQC for this release — expected behavior, documented as a note in E012's acceptance criteria, not a compliance failure.\n114\t- **E005 spans multiple teams**: the strategy delegates per-image switches to component teams via linked tracking tickets; the epic is the coordination wave, and its completion is measured through those tickets plus the payload scan.\n115\t- **Pending process gates**: Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, and Documentation Support are all \"Pending human review\" in the strategy — flagged for human follow-up; no onboarding chain epics were generated because all affected components already have architecture context (in platform).\n116\t- **Out-of-scope boundary honored**: individual-team OCP exception filings, Go ML-KEM / TLSAdherence / TLS-scanner CI (RHAISTRAT-7816), and post-OCP-5.0 base image evolution are excluded from this decomposition.\n117\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bfbd7b3ae971b896","agent_id":"a3c5157a286246483","session_id":"f2d69f1c-9b04-453b-94d6-4bb7c2d36986","uuid":"6518b9e7-78c8-49a5-9cf9-96a5c2158c8d","timestamp":"2026-10-22T07:29:30.047Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-802bd862f05d971d","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
