algorithm evidence from the switched base image for the compliance record.\n44\t- Pure-Rust TLS stack → migrate the data cache's TLS surface to an ML-KEM-capable stack, preserving existing TLS configuration semantics and verifying non-PQC client fallback via classic ECDHE.\n45\t- Either way, document the component's TLS stack and compliance path for the readiness record.\n46\t- If remediation cannot complete by September 15, 2026, the team's OCP exception (TLS stack documented, this remediation as the plan) is the compliance path — filing owned by the team.\n47\t\n48\t## Acceptance Criteria\n49\t\n50\t- The audit outcome is recorded and the component's compliance path is documented.\n51\t- If OpenSSL-backed: evidence shows `update-crypto-policies --show` reporting `DEFAULT:PQ` and ML-KEM algorithms available in the component's switched image.\n52\t- If pure-Rust TLS: the migrated stack negotiates ML-KEM hybrid key exchange with PQC-capable peers and non-PQC clients continue to connect; no HA characteristics change.\n53\t- Until remediated (if required), the team's OCP exception documents the TLS stack and this remediation plan.\n54\t\n55\t## HLR Traceability\n56\t\n57\t- HLR-1 [P0]: All RHOAI runtime images must use a PQC-enabled base image — delivers or verifies ML-KEM capability for this component per the audit outcome.\n58\t- HLR-2 [P0]: Teams unable to comply by September 15 must file an OCP exception — the exception path applies if remediation is needed but not complete.\n59\t- NFR (Security — PQC algorithm compliance, non-Go components): any Rust component using a pure-Rust TLS stack must support ML-KEM hybrid key exchange after migration.\n60\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bfbd7b3ae971b896","agent_id":"a3c5157a286246483","session_id":"f2d69f1c-9b04-453b-94d6-4bb7c2d36986","uuid":"d2ab88c4-7574-4d9d-b2af-50c2fa9e234c","timestamp":"2026-10-22T07:29:50.267Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a6cd31cbcb497daa","permission_decision":{"decision":"