t with the strategy's own framing (September 15 compliance for these components is met via the HLR-2 exception path; the migrations are the remediations those exceptions commit to). No priority collapse: no epic bundles HLRs at multiple priority levels where the lower-priority HLRs are deferrable features.\n\n### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\nCross-check of the summary diagram against epic frontmatter is an exact match in both directions: 5 gate edges (E001→E008, E001→E009, E002→E011, E003→E006, E003→E007) and 11 Rule 11 docs edges (E004–E014 → E015), no extra or missing entries, arrows drawn dependency→dependent per convention. No circular or invalid edges. All blocking edges are justified: investigation gates under Rule 3 with true `remove`/`add_remediation` semantics; the absence of E004→E005 is correctly justified as a configuration reference (Rule 13) enforced by AC gates; E012 and E013 as roots are justified under Rule 15 (verification/performance tooling built in parallel, first meaningful execution gated by their own ACs — E013's before/after structure genuinely delivers independent value via before-measurements and threshold agreement). Tiered delivery (Tier 1 immediate / Tier 2 gated on E001/E003 outcomes) is correctly applied per Rules 17–18. No konflux-onboarding, license-validation, or repo-onboarding epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints are not applicable. Critical path length 3 (Investigation → Implementation → docs) slightly exceeds the S heuristic (1–2), but the strategy's S label understates its span (all component images, multiple teams, three genuine unknowns — effectively M scale), and the path is structurally forced by real investigations plus Rule 11 docs-last. No missing data/artifact edges found in epic content: pipeline consumability and post-switch measurement sequencing are consistently modeled as AC gates, which matches Rules 13/15.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (1/2)\nThree minor issues (3+ minors cost 1 point):\n- E005 bundles th