nly gates. All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts. Modeling the bounded switch-vs-exception outcomes via `gate_failure_impact` rather than conditional branch files is acceptable here because the fallback (team-owned exception filing) is explicitly out of strategy scope, so there is no in-strategy branch work to place in a branch file.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\nType-appropriate signal fields are present on all 15 epics (Investigation epics carry `investigation_signals`, Implementation epics carry `ai_signals`, no cross-contamination), and all 15 ai-signals rationale files exist with rationales matching the frontmatter values. E003's signals (source_accessibility 0, local_runnability 0, human_judgment -1 for a schedule/coordination fact) are exemplary. One arguable value: E001 marks `source_accessibility: 1`, but per the strategy all four variants (python-311, python-312, python-312-minimal, nodejs-22) are \"PQC variant TBD\" — so the registry check can only confirm \"not available now\", and the decisive part of the verdict (committed delivery date on or before September 15) is a pending AIPCC commitment the epic's own scope says must be \"obtained from the AIPCC team\", not read from a source. The rationale's claim that the pending commitments are \"on the periphery\" understates this; 0 (with the registry check as the accessible portion) would be more consistent with E003's treatment of the analogous rebase-timing fact. All other signal values are consistent with rubric conditions and strategy content.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (0/2)\nTwo major issues (missing rule-mandated ACs, Rule 23: replacement → rollback/feature-flag AC), forcing 0:\n- E009 (dashboard nodejs-22 base switch) has no rollback AC. It is the same category of replacement epic as E005–E008, all four of which carry an explicit \"Rollback path: the previous base reference remains restorable\" AC — the mandate is established by the decomposition's own treatment and simply not applied to E009. E009 also lacks the \"PQC base image consumable through the RHOAI build pipeline (build pipeline gate)\" AC its conditional sibling E008 carries, making its AC set thin relative to the pattern.\n- E010 (fms-guardrails-orchestrator TLS stack migration) replaces the existing rustls+ring TLS stack with an ML-KEM-capable provider across all architectures — a replacement of existing capability — and has no rollback/feature-flag AC (revert-to-previous-provider path or a disable flag for the new provider).\nMinor: E011's pure-Rust migration branch likewise has no rollback AC (acceptable to scope it to the migration path only, but it should exist there). Otherwise ACs are testable and well-derived: E012 implements the strategy's AC-1 measurement commands verbatim with the non-OpenSSL exclusion and FIPS caveat; E013 correctly converts the \"NEEDS DOMAIN INPUT\" performance threshold into a documented-agreement AC rather than a soft schedule constraint; E014 encodes the zero-unaccounted-images guarantee; E015 has the Rule 24 technical-review-against-implementation AC. No draft-ID references to sibling epics in any epic body.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\nEvery image group in the strategy's base image mapping has an epic: ubi9-minimal (~68) → E005; rhel9-6-els (~34, notebooks/workbenches/runtimes/mlserver) → E006/E007; python variants (10) → E008; nodejs-22 → E009; openjdk-17/TrustyAI → deliberately no remediation epic (exception expected per strategy, JDK 29 timeline), tracked via E014 and surfaced in Health Warnings — the right call, not a silent drop. All three strategy ACs are covered (AC-1 → E012, AC-2/AC-3 → E014). All four NFRs carried (Security → E010/E011 ACs, backwards compatibility → hybrid/ECDHE fallback ACs, Availability/HA → E010 and E013 ACs, Performance → E013). All three strategy risks and all three open questions map to investigations or E014. Out-of-scope boundaries (RHAISTRAT-7816, post-5.0 evolution, team-owned exception 