{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a6647f92e75d60cc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8001\n3\tscore: 10\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: major\n8\t  criterion: Acceptance Criteria Quality\n9\t  description: E009 (dashboard nodejs-22 base switch) is missing the Rule 23 rollback/feature-flag\n10\t    AC that all four sibling switch epics E005-E008 carry (previous base reference\n11\t    remains restorable); it also lacks the build-pipeline-gate AC that E008 carries.\n12\t    Add both ACs.\n13\t- severity: major\n14\t  criterion: Acceptance Criteria Quality\n15\t  description: E010 (fms-guardrails-orchestrator TLS stack migration) replaces the\n16\t    existing rustls+ring stack with an ML-KEM-capable provider across all architectures\n17\t    but has no rollback/feature-flag AC (Rule 23). Add a revert-to-previous-provider\n18\t    or disable-flag AC.\n19\t- severity: minor\n20\t  criterion: Acceptance Criteria Quality\n21\t  description: E011 pure-Rust TLS migration branch has no rollback AC; scope one to\n22\t    the migration path.\n23\t- severity: minor\n24\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n25\t  description: 'E001 source_accessibility: 1 is arguable — all four variants are TBD\n26\t    per the strategy, so the decisive verdict (committed delivery date) is a pending\n27\t    AIPCC commitment obtained by asking the team, not read from a source; 0 would\n28\t    be consistent with E003 treatment of the analogous rebase-timing fact.'\n29\t- severity: minor\n30\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n31\t  description: E005 and E014 (same team) both maintain per-image completion status\n32\t    via linked tracking tickets; fold the E005 wave-status tracking into the single\n33\t    aggregated compliance view owned by E014.\n34\t- severity: minor\n35\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n36\t  description: E005 bundles the ~68-image switch wave across dozens of teams into\n37\t    one DevOps Build epic; defensible under the strategy ticket-delegation model and\n38\t    flagged in Health Warnings, but oversized — consider splitting by image family\n39\t    or explicitly subordinating execution to team tickets in the ACs.\n40\t- severity: minor\n41\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n42\t  description: E004 scope bullet to track the AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase duplicates the\n43\t    entire purpose of E003; E004 should consume the E003 go/no-go outcome instead\n44\t    of re-tracking AIPCC-7258.\n45\terror: null\n46\t---\n47\t## Review Summary\n48\t\n49\tScore: 10/14 — fail\n50\tRecommendation: revise\n51\t\n52\tContract validator: OK (body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, and priority inheritance all pass). Triage consistency: `triage` is null (full decomposition) with 15 epics — consistent. The decomposition is structurally sound: the DAG is acyclic and internally consistent, all three Investigations are true gates, HLR coverage is complete, and strategy context is carried forward with unusual care (Health Warnings capture the TrustyAI gap, the P1 judgment call, and the FIPS caveat). The failure is narrow: rule-mandated rollback/feature-flag acceptance criteria are missing on the epics that replace existing runtime substrates (Rule 23), after the decomposer itself established that mandate by adding them to E004–E008.\n53\t\n54\t## Criterion Details\n55\t\n56\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n57\tAll three P0 HLRs map to epics: HLR-1 (PQC base for all images) → E004–E012 with per-group switch epics E005–E009 plus remediations E010/E011, verified by E012; HLR-2 (OCP exceptions) → E014 plus exception-fallback ACs on E006, E008, E009, E010, E011; HLR-3 (runtime-stage-only) → E005 primary with the constraint carried in E006–E009 scope. HLR-4 (ML-DSA) is explicitly deferred by the strategy — correctly no epic. Traceability matrix present and accurate. Priority inheritance holds: E001/E002/E003 (P0) gate P0 work; E010/E011 (P1) block no P0 work (only E015, a docs-authoring epic exempt from inheritance). The P1 assignment on E010/E011 despite P0 HLRs is a judgment call, but it is transparently flagged in Health Warnings and is consistent with the strategy's own framing (September 15 compliance for these components is met via the HLR-2 exception path; the migrations are the remediations those exceptions commit to). No priority collapse: no epic bundles HLRs at multiple priority levels where the lower-priority HLRs are deferrable features.\n58\t\n59\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n60\tCross-check of the summary diagram against epic frontmatter is an exact match in both directions: 5 gate edges (E001→E008, E001→E009, E002→E011, E003→E006, E003→E007) and 11 Rule 11 docs edges (E004–E014 → E015), no extra or missing entries, arrows drawn dependency→dependent per convention. No circular or invalid edges. All blocking edges are justified: investigation gates under Rule 3 with true `remove`/`add_remediation` semantics; the absence of E004→E005 is correctly justified as a configuration reference (Rule 13) enforced by AC gates; E012 and E013 as roots are justified under Rule 15 (verification/performance tooling built in parallel, first meaningful execution gated by their own ACs — E013's before/after structure genuinely delivers independent value via before-measurements and threshold agreement). Tiered delivery (Tier 1 immediate / Tier 2 gated on E001/E003 outcomes) is correctly applied per Rules 17–18. No konflux-onboarding, license-validation, or repo-onboarding epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints are not applicable. Critical path length 3 (Investigation → Implementation → docs) slightly exceeds the S heuristic (1–2), but the strategy's S label understates its span (all component images, multiple teams, three genuine unknowns — effectively M scale), and the path is structurally forced by real investigations plus Rule 11 docs-last. No missing data/artifact edges found in epic content: pipeline consumability and post-switch measurement sequencing are consistently modeled as AC gates, which matches Rules 13/15.\n61\t\n62\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (1/2)\n63\tThree minor issues (3+ minors cost 1 point):\n64\t- E005 bundles the ~68-image ubi9-minimal switch wave — work owned by dozens of component teams — into one DevOps Build epic. Defensible given the strategy's own delegation model (per-image execution lives in team-owned tracking tickets, and exception filing is explicitly out of scope), and flagged in Health Warnings, but the epic's completion is genuinely multi-sprint and its \"component\" (OpenShift AI Productization) is a catch-all for many teams' images.\n65\t- Scope duplication between E005 and E014 (same team, DevOps Build): E005's scope includes \"Track per-image completion status through team tracking tickets\" while E014 owns \"the aggregated compliance view: per image, switched / in-progress / exception-filed / unaccounted\" over the same linked-ticket surface. The wave-status tracking should fold into E014's single aggregated view rather than being maintained twice.\n66\t- E004's scope bullet \"Track the AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase for the rhel9-8-els consumption path\" duplicates E003's entire reason for existing (tracking AIPCC-7258 timing and producing the go/no-go). E004 should consume E003's outcome, not re-track it.\n67\tNo work-product type mixing (E015 is pure docs-authoring; no epic bundles code with content authoring). E007/E008 image sets are disjoint (rhel9-6-els runtimes vs python-variant images). Component names for E002/E011 (Model Customization, no canonical \"Trainer\") and E007/E008 (Model Runtimes spanning feature-server/lmes-job) are documented judgment calls, acceptable.\n68\t\n69\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n70\tAll three Investigations are genuine: E001's variant verdict determines whether E008/E009 exist (`remove`), E002's audit determines E011's scope (`add_remediation`), E003's go/no-go determines whether E006/E007 exist (`remove`). Each downstream epic listing an Investigation in `dependencies` has `gated_by` set, every `gated_by` target is in the direct dependency list, and every `gate_failure_impact` has both `action` and a concrete `fallback_approach` — no scheduling-only gates. All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts. Modeling the bounded switch-vs-exception outcomes via `gate_failure_impact` rather than conditional branch files is acceptable here because the fallback (team-owned exception filing) is explicitly out of strategy scope, so there is no in-strategy branch work to place in a branch file.\n71\t\n72\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n73\tType-appropriate signal fields are present on all 15 epics (Investigation epics carry `investigation_signals`, Implementation epics carry `ai_signals`, no cross-contamination), and all 15 ai-signals rationale files exist with rationales matching the frontmatter values. E003's signals (source_accessibility 0, local_runnability 0, human_judgment -1 for a schedule/coordination fact) are exemplary. One arguable value: E001 marks `source_accessibility: 1`, but per the strategy all four variants (python-311, python-312, python-312-minimal, nodejs-22) are \"PQC variant TBD\" — so the registry check can only confirm \"not available now\", and the decisive part of the verdict (committed delivery date on or before September 15) is a pending AIPCC commitment the epic's own scope says must be \"obtained from the AIPCC team\", not read from a source. The rationale's claim that the pending commitments are \"on the periphery\" understates this; 0 (with the registry check as the accessible portion) would be more consistent with E003's treatment of the analogous rebase-timing fact. All other signal values are consistent with rubric conditions and strategy content.\n74\t\n75\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (0/2)\n76\tTwo major issues (missing rule-mandated ACs, Rule 23: replacement → rollback/feature-flag AC), forcing 0:\n77\t- E009 (dashboard nodejs-22 base switch) has no rollback AC. It is the same category of replacement epic as E005–E008, all four of which carry an explicit \"Rollback path: the previous base reference remains restorable\" AC — the mandate is established by the decomposition's own treatment and simply not applied to E009. E009 also lacks the \"PQC base image consumable through the RHOAI build pipeline (build pipeline gate)\" AC its conditional sibling E008 carries, making its AC set thin relative to the pattern.\n78\t- E010 (fms-guardrails-orchestrator TLS stack migration) replaces the existing rustls+ring TLS stack with an ML-KEM-capable provider across all architectures — a replacement of existing capability — and has no rollback/feature-flag AC (revert-to-previous-provider path or a disable flag for the new provider).\n79\tMinor: E011's pure-Rust migration branch likewise has no rollback AC (acceptable to scope it to the migration path only, but it should exist there). Otherwise ACs are testable and well-derived: E012 implements the strategy's AC-1 measurement commands verbatim with the non-OpenSSL exclusion and FIPS caveat; E013 correctly converts the \"NEEDS DOMAIN INPUT\" performance threshold into a documented-agreement AC rather than a soft schedule constraint; E014 encodes the zero-unaccounted-images guarantee; E015 has the Rule 24 technical-review-against-implementation AC. No draft-ID references to sibling epics in any epic body.\n80\t\n81\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n82\tEvery image group in the strategy's base image mapping has an epic: ubi9-minimal (~68) → E005; rhel9-6-els (~34, notebooks/workbenches/runtimes/mlserver) → E006/E007; python variants (10) → E008; nodejs-22 → E009; openjdk-17/TrustyAI → deliberately no remediation epic (exception expected per strategy, JDK 29 timeline), tracked via E014 and surfaced in Health Warnings — the right call, not a silent drop. All three strategy ACs are covered (AC-1 → E012, AC-2/AC-3 → E014). All four NFRs carried (Security → E010/E011 ACs, backwards compatibility → hybrid/ECDHE fallback ACs, Availability/HA → E010 and E013 ACs, Performance → E013). All three strategy risks and all three open questions map to investigations or E014. Out-of-scope boundaries (RHAISTRAT-7816, post-5.0 evolution, team-owned exception filings, ML-DSA) are honored. The docs-authoring epic (E015) covers the strategy's planned PQC Readiness Matrix design doc; Documentation Support is \"Pending human review\" rather than \"Yes\", so including E015 is conservative, not a violation, and all pending process gates are flagged for human follow-up in Health Warnings.\n83\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b1e60a45d5b7e82d","agent_id":"a9d1a007d1727a349","session_id":"f2d69f1c-9b04-453b-94d6-4bb7c2d36986","uuid":"fb39b804-b988-4d80-b6a0-9a27638d1fa8","timestamp":"2026-10-22T07:35:51.624Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a6647f92e75d60cc","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
