{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-85dc681ab5657a45","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8001-E002\n3\ttitle: Audit trainer data cache Rust TLS stack posture\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8001\n5\tcomponent: Model Customization\n6\tteam: Model Training\n7\ttype: Investigation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tinvestigation_signals:\n11\t  question_specificity: 1\n12\t  source_accessibility: 1\n13\t  local_runnability: 1\n14\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: 0\n15\t  human_judgment_required: 0\n16\timplementation_type: null\n17\tai_signals: null\n18\tai_implementability: High\n19\tai_implementability_score: 3\n20\tjira_key: null\n21\tbranch: null\n22\tgated_by: null\n23\tgate_failure_impact: null\n24\t---\n25\t# Audit trainer data cache Rust TLS stack posture\n26\t\n27\t## Description\n28\t\n29\tDetermine the TLS stack posture of the trainer data cache component: whether it uses system OpenSSL via a native-tls stack (and therefore inherits PQC from a PQC-enabled base image) or a pure-Rust TLS stack such as rustls with the ring provider (which cannot inherit PQC from a base image swap and would need remediation or an OCP exception). This resolves the unknown tracked by audit spike RHOAIENG-7196.\n30\t\n31\t## Scope\n32\t\n33\t- Audit the trainer data cache source and dependency tree (Cargo dependency graph, TLS client/server construction sites) to identify the concrete TLS stack in use.\n34\t- Classify the outcome: OpenSSL/native-tls backed (base image switch is sufficient) versus pure-Rust TLS stack (remediation or exception required).\n35\t- If a pure-Rust TLS stack is found, characterize the migration surface (which crates and call sites) so the remediation can be scoped.\n36\t- Document the finding and the resulting compliance path for the component.\n37\t\n38\t## Acceptance Criteria\n39\t\n40\t- A written audit record identifies the TLS stack of the trainer data cache with concrete evidence (dependency and source references).\n41\t- The record states one of: PQC inherited from the PQC base image (no remediation needed), or remediation required (with the affected crates/call sites enumerated).\n42\t- The finding is handed to the component team and the compliance coordination effort.\n43\t\n44\t## HLR Traceability\n45\t\n46\t- HLR-1 [P0]: All RHOAI runtime images must use a PQC-enabled base image — this audit determines whether the base image switch actually delivers ML-KEM capability for this component.\n47\t- HLR-2 [P0]: Teams unable to comply by September 15 must file an OCP exception — the audit outcome decides whether this component is on the switch path or the exception path.\n48\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8f9b93b48fa20659","agent_id":"ab172bc4e60960384","session_id":"f2d69f1c-9b04-453b-94d6-4bb7c2d36986","uuid":"16ec5d4d-5673-4f2f-97ac-7c8ab38a08b3","timestamp":"2026-10-22T07:38:14.289Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-85dc681ab5657a45","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
