{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-97812d75e6b4e94b","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8001-E010\n3\ttitle: fms-guardrails-orchestrator ML-KEM TLS stack migration\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8001\n5\tcomponent: AI Guardrails\n6\tteam: AI Guardrails\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P1\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 0\n12\t  pattern_precedent: -1\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n14\t  existing_foundation: 1\n15\t  open_questions: -1\n16\t  external_dependency: -1\n17\t  human_process_gates: 0\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 1\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tinvestigation_signals: null\n22\tai_implementability: Medium\n23\tai_implementability_score: 0\n24\tjira_key: null\n25\tbranch: null\n26\tgated_by: null\n27\tgate_failure_impact: null\n28\t---\n29\t# fms-guardrails-orchestrator ML-KEM TLS stack migration\n30\t\n31\t## Description\n32\t\n33\tMigrate the fms-guardrails-orchestrator service off its rustls+ring TLS stack so it can negotiate ML-KEM (FIPS 203) hybrid key exchange, as required by the strategy's security NFR for non-Go components using pure-Rust TLS stacks. The service cannot inherit PQC from a base image swap because OpenSSL is not present in its release image; the migration is the remediation path behind the TLS migration spike RHOAIENG-7452. Until the migration lands, the team's September 15, 2026 compliance is met via an OCP exception with this migration as the remediation plan.\n34\t\n35\t## Scope\n36\t\n37\t- Resolve the TLS migration approach per the RHOAIENG-7452 spike outcome (crate/provider selection for an ML-KEM-capable Rust TLS stack, e.g. an aws-lc-rs-backed rustls provider or equivalent).\n38\t- Migrate the service's TLS surfaces (inbound TLS/mTLS on the ingress surface, downstream REST and gRPC client connections) to the ML-KEM-capable stack, across all build variants (amd64, ppc64le, s390x).\n39\t- Preserve existing TLS configuration semantics (per-service certificate and CA bundles, optional TLS/mTLS flags, header passthrough behavior).\n40\t- Verify hybrid key exchange backwards compatibility: non-PQC TLS 1.3 clients continue to connect via classic ECDHE.\n41\t- If the migration cannot complete by September 15, 2026, the OCP exception (with this migration as the documented remediation plan) remains the compliance path — the exception filing itself is owned by the team.\n42\t\n43\t## Acceptance Criteria\n44\t\n45\t- The service's release image uses an ML-KEM-capable TLS stack on all architectures; hybrid X25519MLKEM768 key exchange is negotiated with PQC-capable peers.\n46\t- Non-PQC TLS 1.3 clients continue to connect (opportunistic hybrid negotiation, classic ECDHE fallback verified).\n47\t- Existing TLS/mTLS configuration options behave as before (no HA or configuration regressions; no additional replicas required).\n48\t- Until merged, the team's OCP exception documents this migration as the remediation plan, including the rustls+ring TLS stack as the reason ML-KEM cannot currently be negotiated.\n49\t- Rollback path: the previous rustls+ring TLS stack remains restorable (revert to the previous provider configuration), verified by restoring the prior stack and confirming the service's TLS/mTLS surfaces still function.\n50\t\n51\t## HLR Traceability\n52\t\n53\t- HLR-1 [P0]: All RHOAI runtime images must use a PQC-enabled base image — this epic delivers the \"or equivalent\" ML-KEM capability for a component a base image swap cannot remediate.\n54\t- HLR-2 [P0]: Teams unable to comply by September 15 must file an OCP exception — the exception's remediation plan is this migration.\n55\t- NFR (Security — PQC algorithm compliance, non-Go components): fms-guardrails-orchestrator must support ML-KEM hybrid key exchange after migration.\n56\t\n57\t## Risks\n58\t\n59\t- The migration approach depends on the RHOAIENG-7452 spike outcome and on ML-KEM support maturity in the Rust TLS ecosystem (external dependency).\n60\t- Multi-architecture builds (ppc64le, s390x) constrain provider selection.\n61\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8f9b93b48fa20659","agent_id":"ab172bc4e60960384","session_id":"f2d69f1c-9b04-453b-94d6-4bb7c2d36986","uuid":"b0e3811d-3451-4cf5-9661-873e31f61e0b","timestamp":"2026-10-22T07:38:28.195Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-97812d75e6b4e94b","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
